E('3.5-10','Testing biological washing powders',
 'Compare washing treatments using equally stained fabric, equal solution volumes and controlled washing time, agitation and temperature.',
 'An enzyme contribution needs a suitable comparison such as a matched preparation with inactive enzyme; different commercial powders can also differ in non-enzyme ingredients.',
 'Decide how stains will be scored before washing, for example by comparing the colour left on each cloth with a set of standard samples, in the same lighting and by an observer who does not know which treatment each cloth had. Cut the fabric into equal squares, stain each with the same amount of the same stain, and repeat the treatment on several squares. Use the same volume of solution, the same time, the same movement of the fabric and the same temperature.\n\nTo test the enzyme itself, compare a solution of the powder with a matched solution in which the enzyme has been denatured by boiling and cooled again. To test the effect of temperature, keep the powder concentration the same, because hot water also helps to dissolve stains without any enzyme. One powder on one stain does not show how well it works on every stain. Use only safe materials chosen by the teacher.')
C('3.5-10','SYNTH','Describe a complete investigation of the enzyme contribution of a biological washing preparation on a safe teacher-provided protein stain.','Stain equal squares of fabric with the same amount of protein stain. Wash some in the powder solution and others in the same solution after it has been boiled and cooled to denature the enzyme. Keep the volume, temperature, washing time and agitation the same. Score the stain left on each square by comparing it with a set of standard colour samples. Use several squares for each treatment and calculate a mean score.',['1 mark: the independent variable changed is active enzyme compared with enzyme denatured by boiling.','1 mark: the dependent variable measured is the amount of stain left on each square.','1 mark: at least two of these kept the same: amount of stain, volume of solution, temperature, washing time, agitation.','1 mark: the stain left is scored against a set of standard colour samples.','1 mark: several squares are used for each treatment and a mean score is calculated.'])
C('3.5-10','LIM','Suggest an improvement if one wash treatment uses a heavily stained square and the other a lightly stained square.','Standardise the initial stain amount and fabric area. Initial stain differences otherwise confound comparison of cleaning.',['1 mark: the heavily stained square has more stain to remove, so the stain left is not a fair measure of cleaning; this error favours the lightly stained square.','1 mark: an improvement is to put the same amount of stain on the same size of fabric each time.'])
C('3.5-10','APP','A biological powder removes a protein stain. Suggest why this does not show that the same enzyme removes an oily stain equally well.','The enzyme may be a protease with specificity for protein substrates. Oil is a different material and may need a lipase or other cleaning action.',['1 mark: a protease acts only on protein, because enzymes are specific to their substrate.','1 mark: this oily stain is fat, so it needs a lipase, which the powder may not contain.'])

summaries={
 '3.1':'Magnification is a ratio: image size divided by actual size, in the same unit. Actual size is a length with a unit, not a value written with ×. Total magnification is eyepiece times objective. Locate a microscope specimen at low power, centre it, and only then increase the power.',
 '3.2':'| Structure | Role/position |\n|---|---|\n| Wall | outside the plant membrane; support |\n| Membrane | boundary controlling exchange |\n| Cytoplasm | many reactions |\n| Nucleus | genetic information |\n| Chloroplast | photosynthesis |\n| Vacuole | cell sap |\n| Ribosome | protein synthesis |\n| Rough ER/vesicle | protein handling/transport |\n| Mitochondrion | aerobic respiration |\n\nA palisade cell and liver cell share membrane, cytoplasm and nucleus; the liver cell has no cellulose wall or chloroplasts. Draw what you can see, with clear lines and labels. Cells form tissues, tissues form organs and organs form organ systems.',
 '3.3.1':'Diffusion is net movement down a concentration gradient from random particle motion, and it needs no energy from respiration. A larger area, a steeper gradient, higher temperature and shorter distance each make exchange faster when the other conditions are the same. Random motion continues at equilibrium. Investigations need a fixed endpoint and controlled variables.',
 '3.4':'| Larger substance | Components/example role |\n|---|---|\n| Starch/glycogen | simple sugar units; storage |\n| Protein | amino acids; structure and specialised functions |\n| Fat/oil | fatty acids and glycerol; storage/insulation |\n| DNA | paired strands in a double helix; A–T and C–G |\n\nTests: Benedict’s plus safe heating for reducing sugar; iodine for starch; Biuret for protein; ethanol emulsion for lipid; DCPIP decolourisation for vitamin C. Test separate portions and use equal amounts when comparing.',
 '3.5':'An enzyme is a protein catalyst. A suitable substrate binds at the active site, products form and leave, and the enzyme is unchanged. Temperature and pH affect activity: above the optimum an enzyme is denatured, and its active site changes shape, whereas cooling only slows the reaction. Use matched concentrations, conditions, endpoints and repeats in enzyme investigations.'}
